\chapter{Who Runs Next?}
\label{ch:concurrency}
\index{scheduling}\index{concurrency}\index{interrupts}\index{locking}

\section{Concurrency begins before a second processor}

A machine with one processor can still have concurrent activity.
A process may be suspended while another runs; a device can complete an
operation while code is manipulating a related structure. The execution
is interleaved rather than physically simultaneous, but the program must
still account for more than one possible ordering.

This distinction is essential when reading Lions. Sixth Edition's
uniprocessor kernel could rely on the fact that ordinary kernel execution
was not preempted by another process at an arbitrary C statement.
A process gave up the processor at explicit points, especially when
sleeping. Interrupt handlers were a different source of interleaving and
required their own discipline \parencite[sec. 8.16]{lions}.

An algorithm correct under these assumptions can fail immediately on a
multiprocessor. Disabling interrupts on one core does not prevent another
core from changing shared memory. Nor does the absence of an explicit
sleep guarantee that a second core is not already inside the same function.
Historical code must be read with its execution model attached.

\section{A context switch preserves a continuation}
\index{context switching}

A scheduler chooses runnable work. A context switch makes that choice
real by saving one execution context and restoring another. Registers,
stack state, address-space state, and bookkeeping all contribute to the
ability to continue a suspended computation.

Not every switch saves every piece of state in the same place.
A trap may already have saved user registers in a trap frame. A kernel
context switch may save only the registers needed to resume a kernel
continuation. Confusing these records leads to the false expectation that
one structure contains the entire process at every moment.

In xv6, the relationship among a process's kernel context, its trap frame,
and the per-CPU scheduler context is explicit. The low-level switch
function transfers execution between carefully arranged stacks.
A caller eventually returns along a control path that can look surprising
if the reader assumes every return corresponds to the immediately
preceding chronological call on the same CPU \parencite[ch. 6]{xv6}.

A switch also costs more than the instructions that save registers.
Caches may contain another process's working set; translation state may
need adjustment; the new process may run on a different core.
Scheduling policy therefore interacts with memory locality and processor
topology. ``Always run the next waiting process immediately'' is not a
complete performance policy.

\section{Runnable is different from running}

A process waiting for disk input cannot use the CPU productively merely
because its priority is high. It needs an event to make progress.
A runnable process has work it can execute; a running process is currently
executing; a sleeping process waits for some condition. Real kernels use
more detailed states, but this distinction is fundamental.

Scheduling policies balance responsiveness, throughput, fairness,
accounting, and sometimes deadlines. These goals can conflict.
Giving interactive work prompt service can improve perceived performance
while delaying a long computation. Keeping a task on one core can improve
locality while leaving another core temporarily less busy.

Historical Unix scheduling used priorities and feedback related to CPU
use. Production systems have repeatedly revised these mechanisms as
hardware and workloads changed. It is safer to explain the policy's goals
than to treat one version's formula as the definition of Unix scheduling.
The same applies to claims that one scheduler is universally ``fair'':
fairness requires a stated unit, population, and time scale.

\section{The lost wakeup is a missing agreement}
\index{sleep and wakeup}\index{lost wakeups}

Suppose a consumer needs an item from an empty queue. It checks the queue,
then prepares to sleep. A producer inserts an item and sends a wakeup in
between those two actions. If wakeups are not remembered and the consumer
has not yet registered its sleep, the notification reaches nobody.
The consumer then sleeps despite work being available.

The problem is not that the producer forgot to send a notification.
It is that checking the condition and registering the wait were not
coordinated with the producer's update. Thompson's implementation paper
explains that event wakeups do not themselves retain a count of past
events. The surrounding protocol must close the race
\parencite[sec. 2.3]{implementation}.

The usual conceptual pattern is:

\begin{verbatim}
lock the condition's lock
while the condition is false:
    sleep, atomically releasing that lock
    resume with that lock held again
perform the protected operation
unlock
\end{verbatim}

This is pseudocode. A concrete sleep primitive must define precisely how
it transfers protection between the condition lock and the bookkeeping
that records the sleeping process. Xv6 demonstrates such a protocol using
process locks and a channel associated with the event
\parencite[sec. 6.5]{xv6}.

The loop is important. A wakeup means the condition may have changed,
not that this particular waiter owns the resource. Another waiter can win
the race, several waiters can be awakened, or a more general interface can
permit spurious wakeups. The condition is the truth; the notification is
an invitation to check it again.

\section{Locks protect invariants}
\index{spinlocks}\index{deadlock}\index{memory ordering}

A lock does not make a variable correct by proximity. It protects an
invariant maintained by all code participating in the protocol.
For a queue, the invariant might connect a head pointer, tail pointer,
item count, and links. Updating only one field under the lock while
reading related fields without protection can still expose an impossible
state.

Spinlocks wait by repeatedly attempting acquisition. They are appropriate
only when the holder will release promptly and the waiting context can
afford to spin. A sleeping lock allows the waiter to relinquish the CPU,
but cannot be used indiscriminately in interrupt context or while holding
resources whose progress requires that same CPU.

Atomic machine instructions make acquisition indivisible, but hardware
and compilers can reorder ordinary memory operations. Acquire and release
semantics establish the ordering needed for one core to observe changes
made under the lock by another. An implementation that works accidentally
on one processor's memory model can fail on a weaker one.

Deadlock appears when dependencies form a cycle: one participant holds A
while waiting for B, another holds B while waiting for A. A consistent
lock order prevents many such cycles. Avoiding sleep while holding certain
locks prevents others. Lock ordering must be documented at subsystem
boundaries, where a locally reasonable acquisition can complete a global
cycle nobody intended.

Xv6 deliberately exposes these issues. Its small size lets the reader
follow where a lock is acquired, why interrupts are controlled, and how
ownership may be transferred across scheduling. Modern kernels add more
specialized mechanisms, including read-copy-update and lock-free
structures, but those techniques still require explicit lifetime and
ordering arguments.

\section{The hard part is preserving progress}

Mutual exclusion alone does not guarantee useful behavior.
A process can starve if other work repeatedly overtakes it. A low-priority
holder can delay high-priority work, producing priority inversion.
A wakeup strategy can awaken many processes that mostly return to sleep,
creating a thundering herd. Performance failures can arise from a protocol
that is technically free of data races.

The best starting point is to state both safety and progress properties.
Safety might say that an object is never freed while a user holds a
reference. Progress might say that a waiting operation eventually receives
a chance to run when its resource becomes available. Each needs different
evidence. Stress testing can reveal violations, but a successful run is
not a proof that all interleavings are safe.

This is why concurrency occupies so much of kernel engineering.
The visible interface often promises a simple result, while the
implementation must maintain that result across interruptions, scheduling,
and simultaneous execution. The complexity is concentrated around the
moments when one computation depends on another. Understanding those
moments makes the rest of the kernel much easier to read.
